  "ev.wwx":"White wins",
  "op.study":"Study (a typical line)",
  "op.studyDesc":"{n} moves of a typical line analysed by the AI, one at a time with the evaluation",
  "op.practice":"Practice",
  "op.practiceDesc":"5 \"next move\" problems from positions of this opening, scored by the AI's evaluation",
  "op.studyStart":"First look at the shape of the first three moves. \"Next\" plays one move at a time.",
  "op.studyEnd":"That is the end of the line. Go on to \"Practice\".",
  "op.goal":"What is the best next move?",
  "op.hint":"The golden dot is where the AI's best move is.",
  "op.good":"{m} is among the best moves according to the AI.",
  "op.better":"The AI's best move was {m} (you played {x}). The numbers are the AI's evaluation.",
  "op.pass":"Opening master!",
  "op.credit":"Opening names and evaluations follow the Japan Renju Federation and RIF (evaluations under the old RIF opening rule). The lines and practice problems were analysed by this page's AI and are not necessarily established theory.",
  "op.n.four":"A four — the opponent has to block.",
  "op.n.three":"A three — left alone it becomes a straight four.",
  "op.n.fourthree":"Four-three! The game is decided.",
  "op.n.block4":"Blocks the four.",
  "op.n.block3":"Blocks the opponent's three.",
  "op.n.win":"From here {c} has a forced win.",
  "pz.title":"Renju problems",
  "pz.desc":"500 \"win in N\" renju problems (renju rules). Moves are counted for both sides up to the move that settles the game (a four-three, four-four or straight four). The defender always picks the longest defence. The first move is unique, and every problem was verified by the AI's solver. The daily problem changes every day.",
  "pz.credit":"{n} problems in total, taken from positions in this page's AI self-play and checked with the solver for a unique first move and the shortest win.",
  "pz.winIn":"Win in {n}",
  "pz.goal":"{c} to move: settle the win within {n} moves",
  "pz.failWhy":"{m} does not win within {n} moves. The answer is {b}.",
  "pz.good":"Good move. Waiting for the reply…",
  "pz.yourMove":"Your move ({n} moves left).",
  "pz.solvedSub":"You settled the win in {n} moves! Playing out the finish.",
  "pz.hintText":"A winning move is near the golden dots.",
  "pzc.intro":"Basics",
  "pzc.intro.sub":"Win in one: the basic winning shapes. Four-three or straight four; for White also four-four and three-three.",
  "pzc.vcf":"Fours (VCF)",
  "pzc.vcf.sub":"Win by continuous fours: every reply is forced.",
  "pzc.vct":"Threes & fours (VCT)",
  "pzc.vct.sub":"Attack with threes too. A three can be blocked in several ways, and the defender picks the best one.",
  "pzc.forbid":"Forbidden-point attacks",
  "pzc.forbid.sub":"White to move: win by making Black's blocking point forbidden — a renju speciality.",
  "pzl.intro":"Settle the game in one · {k} problems",
  "pzl.vcf":"{n} moves with fours only · {k} problems",
  "pzl.vct":"{n} moves with threes and fours · {k} problems",
  "pzl.forbid":"{n} moves using forbidden points · {k} problems",
  "rl.title":"Illustrated rules",
  "rl.tab.basic":"Basics",
  "rl.tab.renju":"Forbidden",
  "rl.tab.terms":"Terms",
  "rl.tab.open":"Opening",
  "rl.lead.basic":"Black and White take turns placing one stone on an intersection. The first to make five in a row — across, down or diagonally — wins. Stones are never moved or captured.",
  "rl.board.h":"Board and stones",
  "rl.board":"Play on the 15×15 intersections. Black moves first. Numbering the stones in order gives the game record (the a–o and 1–15 along the edges are the coordinates).",
  "rl.five.h":"To win: five in a row",
  "rl.five":"Make five of your stones in a row, horizontally, vertically or diagonally.",
  "rl.rules3.h":"Three rule sets",
  "rl.rules3":"Freestyle: five or more wins. Standard: exactly five wins (six or more does not). Renju: Black has forbidden moves; Black wins with exactly five, White with five or more. The six black stones shown win in freestyle, but not in standard or renju.",
  "rl.draw.h":"Draw",
  "rl.draw":"If the board fills up without five in a row, the game is a draw (this almost never happens).",
  "rl.lead.renju":"To keep Black's first-move advantage in check, renju gives only Black some forbidden moves. A forbidden move loses. In this app you cannot play them, and they are marked ×. White has no forbidden moves.",
  "rl.f33.h":"3-3 (double three)",
  "rl.f33":"A move that makes two threes at once. A black stone on × would make two threes (vertical and horizontal), so it is forbidden.",
  "rl.f44.h":"4-4 (double four)",
  "rl.f44":"A move that makes two fours at once. Two fours in a single line (the middle point of ●·●●●·●) are also a 4-4.",
  "rl.fol.h":"Overline",
  "rl.fol":"Six or more in a row. Black must make exactly five to win; an overline is forbidden.",
  "rl.f43.h":"A four-three is allowed",
  "rl.f43":"One four plus one three made at the same time is not forbidden — it is Black's basic way to win.",
  "rl.prio.h":"Five comes first",
  "rl.prio":"A move that makes exactly five is never forbidden, even if it also forms a 4-4 or 3-3: Black wins.",
  "rl.white.h":"White has no forbidden moves",
  "rl.white":"White may play 3-3, 4-4 and overlines (six or more also wins). If the point where Black must block is forbidden, Black cannot play there and White wins.",
  "rl.lead.terms":"The shapes that decide games. Green circles mark \"play here and…\" points; grey dots are other places to block.",
  "rl.four.h":"Four",
  "rl.four":"One more stone makes five. It is a four even if one end is blocked. The opponent must block that point.",
  "rl.sfour.h":"Straight four",
  "rl.sfour":"A four that makes five at either end. The opponent cannot block both, so a straight four wins.",
  "rl.three.h":"Three (open three)",
  "rl.three":"Three in a row that becomes a straight four with one more stone. Block it right at an end (green) or one point further out (grey).",
  "rl.split.h":"Split three",
  "rl.split":"A three with one gap. Filling the gap (green) makes a straight four, so it is also a three. Block it in the gap or at an end.",
  "rl.lead.open":"In renju the first three moves are played near the centre of the board. There are 26 possible shapes of the first three moves, called the 26 openings.",
  "rl.open.h":"The first three moves",
  "rl.open":"Move 1 (Black) on the centre point (h8), move 2 (White) next to it, move 3 (Black) within two lines of the centre (grey dots). The renju rules in this app use this restriction.",
  "rl.direct.h":"Direct openings",
  "rl.direct":"The second move is orthogonally next to the centre. Depending on the third move there are 13 openings (Kagetsu, Kansei…).",
  "rl.indirect.h":"Indirect openings",
  "rl.indirect":"The second move is diagonally next to the centre. These also give 13 openings (Hogetsu, Chosei…).",
  "rl.pro.h":"Tournament opening rules",
  "rl.pro":"Many openings are known forced wins for Black, so tournaments add opening rules (choosing openings alternately, offering two fifth moves, RIF's Taraguchi rule…). For casual play this app lets Black choose the first three moves freely. Learn the 26 openings in the \"Openings\" tab.",
